from The Elliott Wave Theorist (an investment tipsheet), 94/9/30
--relayed without permission, usual mumbo-jumbo applies

CULTURAL TRENDS: Hyper-Optimism at a Multi-Century Peak

In the past six years, a new attitude embracing the idea of limitless
human potential has formed under the banner of Extropianism (opposing
entropy, the natural winding-down in nature that occurs without an
infusion of energy).  Its founder refers to the attitude as "_dynamic
optimism_," by which it is understood that technology, reason,
intelligence, energy, vitality, and spontaneous order will bring
boundless progress, achieving for mankind what in the past have only
been impossible dreams.  Much could be said about the ideas
themselves, but this section deals with the _implication_ of cultural
trends.  This recently formed, emotionally charged "gonzo" movement
advocating "dynamic optimism" is occurring at a social mood peak of
Grand Supercycle degree.  This is the time when expressions of the old
bull market sentiment of achievement, attainment, heroism, and
optimism reach their giddy heights.

This is not merely an assertion.  It is more than coincidence that the
very word "optimism" first appeared in a French publication in 1737
describing Leibniz's doctrine that we live in the best of all possible
worlds, which itself was developed in the 17-teens up to 1716, just
four years from the top of the multi-century Grand Supercycle advance
that preceded a 64-year bear market.  The word next appeared in
publications (including _Candide_) _ridiculing_ the idea in 1759, at
the momentum low of a two decade long bear market.  Not surprisingly,
the word "pessimism," reflecting the polar doctrine, first appeared in
a letter (by Coleridge) in 1794, ten years off the Grand Supercycle
bottom, having apparently been in use for several years.  That bottom
was preceded by 64 years of bear market and has been followed by 210
years of bull market.  As you can see on the 300-year stock market
chart below, _these words and doctrines were formulated near the point
of maximum social mood supporting them, and THEREFORE at precisely the
times that the opposite view should have been taken._

Optimists of the early 1700s had to wait decades, as well as through
two revolutions and a depression, before their optimism began to be
borne out.  The Extropians' optimism similarly will be proven
justified, but not until the next Grand Supercycle bull market begins.
That point is years away (for a time estimate, see the Special Report
of March 18, 1994), and at least one depression and dangerous war away
as well.  We'll undoubtedly see ridicule of extropianism, and some new
term expressing its opposite, near the next Grand Supercycle low.
Then you'll know that the extropians' time has arrived.

