From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Sun, 06 Nov 1994 22:54:05 GMT
Subject: [#94-11-109] Democracy [was: BIOSPHERE SURVIVAL]

CurtAdams@aol.com wrote:
>Steve Whitrow (sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk) writes:
>>Democracy is like two wolves and ninety-eight sheep voting
>>on what to have for dinner.  The good news is that there are
>>three or four choices on the menu.  The bad news is that each
>>dish includes mutton [...]
>
>In many countries, more people get money from the government than pay money
>to the government.  2 wolves and 1 sheep is a sadly appropriate analogy.

I question the assumption that the majority of people benefit from
government, compared to no government.  The massive cost of government
would mean that the gainers could only gain a tiny fraction of losses
incurred by the losers.  In Britain, for example, government currently
costs 291.8 billion pounds ($470 billion) per annum, which is about $410
weekly per worker, $160 per capita, or about $220 per eligible voter.
Let's say 51% of voters are better off by $10 weekly, thus the other 49%
are worse off by $459 on average.  Or if 67% are better off by $20 per week,
33% lose by $707.  In fact, a minority profit by much more than $20 per
week, lessening the possibility of the remaining 51% / 67% benefiting and /
or increasing the losers' losses.

The next step is to deduct the cost of obtaining comparable services in a
free society.  For starters, many of the "services" of government are 
services that many would choose to forgo.  Eg, being prohibited from the
right to ingest substances into one's own body or not as the case may be,
and as a side effect being more likely to be shot, mugged, burgled, etc.
Next we have to decide how much prices would be lowered by free competition
in a consumers' market compared to a monopoly in each market (socialised
law and order, socialised charity, socialised currency, socialised
medicine, nanny state, etc).  In one system, consumers may freely choose
which legal agency to sign up with, which security services to use (or opt
for personal defence), who to sell their labour to, where to invest in
order to insure against a rainy day, which charity to support in order to
help the genuinely needy, etc.  The other system has monopoly "service"
providers, these being funded by stolen money seized by common criminals
who run something like a chain letter or pyramid selling scam in that the
institution can only consume wealth, but with the added feature that
participation in the scheme is compulsory.

If we predict prices to drop by less than 50% and allow a little more for
unwanted services this suggests a cost of government per voter of $110
per week, although I'd place a higher value on freedom, and OPEC was able
to quadruple the oil price in 1973.  With the $110 per voter average net
loss, if 67% are better off by $10, then 33% lose $354 per week.  It sounds
quite absurd, but so does putting up with an extortion racket that takes
$21320 annually per worker.  

Maybe the majority could benefit in the short term, before the system
collapses (but we want digital cash to bring about a controlled transition
this time to avoid the tyranny stage of the Greek cycle!).  I suspect that
any studies which count people whose welfare income slightly exceeds their
income tax outlay haven't allowed for all the other costs of government.
Such as paying higher prices for goods and services as a consequence of
corporate taxation, taxes incurred on transportation costs, costs of
complying with regulations, sales taxes added to these prices, possible
special taxes added before the sales tax (e.g. gasoline), and savings
taxes such as inflation.  [It's been calculated that the European
Commission (formerly the "Common Market") alone has cost the average
British worker some 11,000 pounds so far in higher food prices.]  And
these are just the tangible costs.

It's easy to name 3 special minority classes that profit from government:
(i) The establishment elite: sleazy politicians, public sector gravy
train.  (ii) Common criminals: less likely to be caught and unlikely to
receive a deterrent penalty in any case.  (iii) Sponging layabouts: those
who deliberately milk the system.  But I don't think these groups comprise
a majority of voters.  And the overlap between them is considerable, if
not virtually total.

